{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a methodological rule of 'rational action' distinct from 'inductive inference,' where corroboration reports past performance without predicting future probability, thus avoiding the smuggled induction.","counterconditions":["If the reason for preferring the survived theory relies on an expectation that it will continue to survive, induction is smuggled back in.","If 'rational action' cannot be defined without referencing future success probabilities, the distinction collapses."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Semantic compartmentalization: separating the logical status of a theory (unprovable, improbable) from the pragmatic decision rule for acting on it (prefer the one that survived severe tests).","optional_refs":["61fd60a35f344c802ede1fa3a6ec8f48925cda1a32048670275d92f98fa9f579","99a3734a3ba5566b3badd0f59475ca1cf66b1ea1f7823755feaf139caf2e22ee"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories inevitably smuggles induction back into Popper's system because any rationale for acting on past test results presupposes the uniformity of nature or the reliability of memory, which are inductive assumptions.","counterconditions":["If a non-inductive basis for action (e.g., convention, decisionism) can be coherently articulated without assuming future regularity.","If the definition of 'rationality' is restricted solely to logical consistency with past data, ignoring future application."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Reductio ad absurdum of the 'methodological rule': demonstrating that the rule 'prefer X' is irrational unless one assumes 'X is a reliable guide to future success,' which is an inductive claim.","optional_refs":["15adc71678331108a100df74736f0137c90bcd4632e9861aadbb5b03c1583a93","360